// The integer terminal, defined once.
//
// Two grammars need it -- `regimen` and `record` -- and for a while each
// spelled it out for itself. The two spellings admitted the same strings and
// a comment in one of them said so, which is the two-readers class at the
// terminal level: nothing executed the claim, so nothing would notice the day
// one of them was widened and the other was not. A regime is identified by
// the settings that name it, so a number one reader accepts and the other
// refuses is two regimes wearing one name.
//
// Included by both grammars through a second `#[grammar]` attribute rather
// than copied into them, so there is one text to change and no second place
// to forget.
//
// `-0` is not here, deliberately: it and `0` project to the same value, so two
// byte-different documents would name one regime. Narrowing is the permitted
// direction, and this is where the narrowing lives.
integer  = @{ ("-" ~ nonzero) | int_part }

// The parts travel with it. Sharing `integer` alone and leaving each grammar
// its own `int_part` would move the divergence rather than remove it -- the
// fractional rules are built out of these too.
int_part = _{ "0" | nonzero }
nonzero  = _{ ASCII_NONZERO_DIGIT ~ ASCII_DIGIT* }
